<h2>What keyword density means</h2><p>In this tool, single-word density is simply the number of times a term appears divided by the total detected word count. It is a descriptive statistic—not a target score and not a promise of search performance.</p><h2>How to use the analysis</h2><ol><li>Paste the text you want to inspect.</li><li>Choose whether common English stop words should be omitted from the single-word frequency table.</li><li>Review recurring words and phrases for accidental repetition, topic consistency and editing opportunities.</li><li>Rewrite for clarity when repetition sounds unnatural; do not force a percentage.</li></ol><h2>Phrase frequency can reveal content patterns</h2><p>Two- and three-word phrases often reveal repeated product names, locations, claims or sentence patterns more clearly than individual words. That makes phrase frequency useful for quality control, editorial review and detecting boilerplate.</p><h2>Why there is no “ideal density” score</h2><p>Search relevance depends on far more than repeating a term. This tool avoids labeling a density percentage as “good” or “bad” because that would turn a neutral text statistic into a misleading optimization rule.</p>
